      "title": "Identifying Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "79d8e5c0-7f76-5462-be6a-2f5ad6c5a9a8",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x & =-2x-4 \\\\ -2x+2x & =-2x-4+2x \\\\ 0 & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+8 & =6x+8 \\\\ 6x+8-6x & =6x+8-6x \\\\ 8 & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $6x+8=6x+8$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-1 & =4x-1 \\\\ 4x-1-4x & =4x-1-4x \\\\ -1 & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $4x-1=4x-1$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $6x+8=6x+8$; $4x-1=4x-1$ only.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "6fe1dcc4-aa33-5ba6-9fe7-08e9fb622b35",
          ":answer-field/key": "$-2x=-2x-4$",
