    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "0f16a99e-2fac-5929-b09f-a7cdbc2f4d2a",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x-3 & =-4x+7 \\\\ -3x-3+4x & =-4x+7+4x \\\\ x-3 & =7 \\\\ x-3+3 & =7+3 \\\\ x & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $-3x-3=-4x+7$ has the unique solution $x=10.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+6 & =4x+6 \\\\ 4x+6-4x & =4x+6-4x \\\\ 6 & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $4x+6=4x+6$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5x+6 & =-5x \\\\ -5x+6+5x & =-5x+5x \\\\ 6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $4x+6=4x+6$ only.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "1f061174-5a7d-5328-9515-c66a381f56ce",
