      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & =4x+7 \\\\ 4x-4x & =4x+7-4x \\\\ 0 & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-3 & =7x-3 \\\\ 7x-3-7x & =7x-3-7x \\\\ -3 & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $7x-3=7x-3$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+6 & =6x+8 \\\\ 6x+6-6x & =6x+8-6x \\\\ 6 & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $7x-3=7x-3$ only.",
